WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:11.978
The rule is Module 03's, plus one line: a cluster's total size may not exceed the capacity C.

2
00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:16.556
At capacity 2 and K = 2, G01 stops at 3 clusters.

3
00:00:16.556 --> 00:00:25.133
The report: legal, but K is not reached; the stop reason is capacity-blocked.

4
00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:29.200
The four are independent; report them separately.

5
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:36.844
One graph: at capacity 3, AB plus C is 3, legal, giving ABC | DE.

6
00:00:36.844 --> 00:00:43.200
At capacity 2, the highest-affinity AB–C is blocked, so D–E instead.

7
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:49.556
After that both candidates are 3: it stops at AB | C | DE, cut 8.

8
00:00:49.956 --> 00:00:53.889
Is that greedy's fault? First, the lower bound.

9
00:00:53.889 --> 00:00:58.489
Total size 5, at most 2 per cluster: at least 3 clusters.

10
00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:03.289
K = 2 is impossible; no algorithm can do it.

11
00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:08.089
But a bound only proves impossible, never possible.

12
00:01:08.489 --> 00:01:16.844
The original author's greedy_trap: A and B have size 2, capacity 3, and the bound allows two clusters.

13
00:01:16.844 --> 00:01:20.311
Greedy merges the biggest, C–D, first.

14
00:01:20.311 --> 00:01:24.444
Then neither A nor B fits: it stops at 3 clusters.

15
00:01:24.444 --> 00:01:30.133
Yet exhaustive search finds AC | BD: feasible, but greedy is stuck.

16
00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:37.111
The suboptimal graph: greedy merges A–B first, the largest at 10.

17
00:01:37.111 --> 00:01:41.022
Both 9s stay in the cut: 18.

18
00:01:41.022 --> 00:01:46.044
The legal AC | BD cuts only 10 and 1: 11.

19
00:01:46.044 --> 00:01:50.844
Both legal, both reach K; greedy just isn't optimal.

20
00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:56.489
Node sizes change the ending too: set A's size to 2.

21
00:01:56.489 --> 00:02:00.178
After A–B it is full, and C does not fit.

22
00:02:00.178 --> 00:02:06.089
The result becomes AB | CDE, cut 5. Capacity counts area.

23
00:02:06.489 --> 00:02:08.622
Four things to keep.

24
00:02:08.622 --> 00:02:13.089
Filter first, then take the largest; report four items;

25
00:02:13.089 --> 00:02:16.333
a bound above K means impossible;

26
00:02:16.333 --> 00:02:22.467
stuck and not optimal need a comparison with search. Try it in the lab below.
